Path('/app/answer.json').write_text('{"answer": "The parametrization in the exception-raising test provides invalid combinations of import names and app names, and the exception handling in the application location function ensures these cases consistently raise `NoAppException`.\\n\\nThe test is parametrized with 9 invalid combinations (lines 199-215 in `tests/test_cli.py`):\\n- Invalid module paths: `(\\"notanapp.py\\", None)`, `(\\"cliapp/app\\", None)`, `(\\"cliapp.message.txt\\", None)`\\n- Invalid app names: `(\\"cliapp.app\\", \\"notanapp\\")`\\n- Invalid factory calls: `(\\"cliapp.factory\\", \'create_app2(\\"foo\\")\')` (wrong args), `(\\"cliapp.factory\\", \\"create_app(\\")` (syntax error), `(\\"cliapp.factory\\", \\"no_app\\")` (no app returned)\\n- Nested import errors: `(\\"cliapp.importerrorapp\\", None)`\\n\\nThe application location function (`locate_app` in `src/flask/cli.py`, lines 241-264) handles these via:\\n\\n1. Import error handling (lines 244-257): Distinguishes direct import failures (module not found) from nested import errors (errors within the imported module) using traceback depth. Both raise `NoAppException` with appropriate messages.\\n\\n2. App discovery logic (lines 261-264): When `app_name` is `None`, calls `find_best_app` (lines 41-91), which raises `NoAppException` if no Flask app is found. When `app_name` is provided, calls `find_app_by_string` (lines 120-197), which:\\n   - Parses the app name as an AST expression (lines 128-165), raising `NoAppException` for syntax errors\\n   - Checks for attribute existence (lines 167-172), raising `NoAppException` if missing\\n   - Validates function call arguments (lines 176-187), raising `NoAppException` for wrong argument counts\\n   - Ensures the result is a Flask instance (lines 191-197), raising `NoAppException` otherwise\\n\\nThis design ensures that all invalid combinations in the parametrization consistently raise `NoAppException`, making the test effective at verifying error handling across different failure modes."}' + '\n')
